course: everyday
lesson: 503
title: Twins and big families
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Twins are often treated as one unit, «the twins», but each twin is her own person. In Manchester, Ayla and Ece, 9, are twins in a warm, loud Turkish-British family. The teacher calls Ayla «Ece» for the fifth time this week. «I'm the one with the plait, miss.» Ece adds, «And I'm the clever one,» and Ayla kicks her under the table. In London, Amira and Yasmin, 10, have always had matching outfits. Now Amira wants her own style; Yasmin still wants to match. Amira feels guilty for wanting to be different, but it's healthy: twins grow into different people. Asking for different groups, or wearing different things, helps people learn who you are.
explain: Twins compare themselves more than most siblings. In Belfast, Ruaridh makes the football team and Cormac doesn't. At home Cormac won't speak to him. «It's not my fault, ye know,» says Ruaridh, feeling guilty for being happy. Be kind about your win and don't rub it in; Cormac can find his own thing. In Edinburgh, loud Oskar always answers for shy Filip: «He wants chips.» Filip wanted pasta. Let your twin speak for himself. In Texas, Jadyn and Jordyn swap places for a math test: Jadyn takes Jordyn's test. The teacher spots Jadyn's handwriting and both get detention. Swaps are for jokes, not for tests: cheating is serious.
explain: Big families mean sharing everything: rooms, the bathroom, the telly, attention. In Liverpool, Mikey, 9, is one of five kids in a three-bed house and shares a room with two brothers. The bathroom queue at 7:30 is chaos. Mum says, «You've got two minutes, our Mikey, then you're out» («our» before a name is Northern for «my family's»). In Kerry, at tea there are six chips left and five hungry kids. Mam says, «Last one to finish homework gets none.» Seán has never done homework so fast. Big families often turn things into races and deals, and fair rules keep the peace.
explain: In big families, new things are rare and precious. In Georgia, USA, Faith, one of six, never gets a new outfit: everything's a hand-me-down. At Easter her aunt buys her a brand-new dress. «Girl, you ate!» says her big sister (Gen Z slang for «you look amazing»). Faith is so happy she cries. In London, Abena, one of five, can never find a quiet place to read, so she hides in the bath (no water!) with a book and a torch. Big families are loud; kids find clever ways to get quiet, and it's fine to tell your family that a corner is your quiet place.
explain: In Cork, Bridie's family is so big they can't fit in one car, so they drive in two cars to Gran's. Bridie's mate says, «Ye're like a football team!» Bridie grins: «We are, and Da's the ref.» Twins, big families, small families: each has its own normal, its own noise and its own fun.
fact: Twins are often treated as one unit, but each twin is a separate person who needs to be seen.
fact: It is fine for twins to ask for different classes or groups, or to dress differently.
fact: Twins grow into different people, and wanting your own style is healthy.
fact: Twins compare themselves a lot, so one twin's win can feel like the other's loss.
fact: When you win and your twin doesn't, be kind and don't rub it in.
fact: One twin speaking for the other too much can leave the quiet one feeling invisible.
fact: Swapping twins for a joke can be fun, but swapping in a test is cheating and is serious.
fact: Big families share rooms, the bathroom and attention, and fair rules keep the peace.
fact: In big families, hand-me-downs are common and a new thing can feel very special.
fact: Kids in loud homes often find a secret quiet corner, and it's fine to ask for one.
fact: «Our» before a name is Northern English for «my family's», «ye» is Irish for you, and «you ate» is Gen Z slang for you look amazing or did brilliantly.
exercise: The teacher keeps calling Ayla «Ece». What does Ayla say? | «I'm the one with the plait, miss» | «Ece's the naughty one, miss» | «We're the same person, miss» | «It doesn't matter, miss»
exercise: Why does it matter that the teacher gets the twins' names right? | Each twin is her own person | Twins hate their own names | It's a school rule | Ece is cleverer
exercise: Amira wants her own style; Yasmin still wants to match. Is Amira wrong? | No, twins grow into different people | Yes, twins must always match their clothes | Yes, she's hurting Yasmin on purpose | No, but she must stop speaking to Yasmin
exercise: Ruaridh makes the team and Cormac doesn't. What should Ruaridh do? | Be kind and not rub it in | Quit the team so it's fair | Brag about it every day at tea | Ask the coach to drop him
exercise: Why does Cormac take it so hard? | Twins compare themselves a lot | He hates football | Ruaridh cheated | He wanted to be in goal
exercise: Oskar says, «He wants chips.» What did Filip want? | Pasta | Chips | Nothing | Pizza
exercise: What should Oskar do? | Let Filip speak for himself | Always order for him | Order chips for both | Speak louder for him
exercise: Jadyn and Jordyn swapped for a math test. What happened? | Both got detention | Both got top marks | Nobody noticed at all | Only Jordyn got in trouble
exercise: How did the teacher catch them? | Jadyn's handwriting | A camera in the classroom | Jordyn told her straight away | They were wearing name tags
exercise: Mikey's mum says «our Mikey». What does «our» mean here? | My family's | Everyone's | Old | Little
exercise: In Mikey's house, what is chaos at 7:30? | The bathroom queue | The school bus | Breakfast TV | The dog
exercise: Seán's mam says, «Last one to finish homework gets none.» What happens? | Seán does his homework very fast | Seán decides to skip tea for good | Seán eats every one of the chips | Seán refuses to do any homework
exercise: Why does Faith cry at Easter? | She's so happy with a new dress | She hates the colour pink | She wanted hand-me-downs | Her aunt was cross with her
exercise: «Girl, you ate!» What does it mean? | You look amazing | You had your dinner | You're greedy | You're late
exercise: Where does Abena read in peace? | In the empty bath with a torch | On the flat roof above the kitchen | In her mum's car | At the bus stop
exercise: How does Bridie's family get to Gran's? | In two cars | On a coach | On bikes | They walk
exercise: What keeps the peace in big families? | Fair rules | More shouting | Locked doors | No rules at all
test: «twins are basically the same person innit». What's true? | Each twin is her own person | Twins share one brain | Twins always like the same food | Twins can't have different styles
test: «Ruaridh should feel bad, he got in and his twin didn't». What does the lesson say? | He can be happy but kind, without rubbing it in | He should quit the team so his twin feels better | He should pretend he didn't get in at all | He should never play football at home again
test: What would probably happen if Oskar kept answering for Filip? | Filip would keep feeling invisible | Filip would get louder than Oskar | Filip would love it and thank him | Nothing, Filip doesn't mind it
test: «swap with ur twin for the spelling test, no one will know, it's bussin'». What's the problem? | It's cheating, and it's serious | Spelling tests are too easy to swap | Teachers can never tell twins apart | It only counts as cheating in maths
test: In a film, twins dress the same until they're 40 and it's played as a happy ending. What does the lesson add? | Wanting your own style is healthy too | Twins must match their whole lives | Dressing differently ends a friendship | Only one twin is allowed a style
test: ★★★ Seán's family: 6 chips are left, and 4 kids finish homework first. Mam cuts chips in half if needed and shares the 6 equally between the 4. How many chips does each get? | One and a half | One | Two | One and a quarter
test: ★★★ Mikey's house: 5 kids each get 2 minutes in the bathroom, one after the other, starting at 7:30. Mikey goes fourth. When does his turn start? | 7:36 | 7:38 | 7:32 | 7:40
test: «Abena reading in the bath with no water is weird». What does the lesson say? | Kids in loud homes find clever quiet spots | Baths are only for washing, so it's wrong | She should read in the busy kitchen instead | Reading in a bath is dangerous with no water
test: «Faith cried over a dress, so dramatic». Why did it mean so much? | In big families new things are rare | She cries about every little thing | She hated the dress and was upset | Her aunt made her wear it to church
test: «Bridie's family needs two cars, that's so embarrassing». How does Bridie see it? | She grins: they're a team and Da's the ref | She's so ashamed she won't get in either car | She wishes she was an only child at once | She tells her mate to mind her own business
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the individual identity of twins? | Each twin is a separate person deserving to be recognised as such | Twins constitute a single unit and may be addressed interchangeably | Twins must retain identical attire and preferences throughout life | Differences between twins indicate a breakdown in their relationship
exam: Identify the principal objection, as established in the lesson, to twins exchanging places in a school examination. | It constitutes cheating | It is impractical | It confuses the parents | It wastes paper
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the preservation of order in households containing many children. | Fair rules maintain peace | Peace requires that no rules exist | Order is secured solely by raising the voice | Order requires that each child dwell apart
exam: It is given that one twin habitually responds on behalf of the other. Ascertain the probable effect upon the quieter twin. | The quieter twin may feel invisible | The quieter twin will become the more talkative | The quieter twin will be relieved of all duties | The quieter twin will cease to have preferences
